From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ashhart <71890694+ashhart@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:28:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/34] prompt cache: a refused restore tries the next shorter
 kept state

Upstream TensorFold commit by ashhart, ported onto this series (upstream main e8420316396b).

Measured first on main-now with the store's fake family: with a kept state at
7 and another at 4, a 9-token prompt whose longest state the backend refuses
prefilled from 0 (8 tokens), while the 4-token state sat unused. The same holds
in prompt_cache_index_test: a 12-token prompt prefilled 12 instead of 8.

beginUser now drops the refused state (as before) and looks up the prompt again,
so the next shorter kept state is restored in the same request; the pass starts
from its position, and only when no kept state resumes does it prefill from the
start with the first lookup's marks, exactly as main did. Refused states stay
expired, so a later request cannot retry them. No replies change: the restored
prefix is the prompt's own (tokens and canonical spans), and the fake family's
pass sum equals a fresh pass's.

Tests: a fake backend that refuses only the longest state resumes the shorter
one, with equal tokens, one counted failure, no outstanding borrows and the live
state count matching the store's; the index test now refuses the longest state
and checks the shorter one resumes from the same begin, then that refusing
every state drops both and prefills from 0. test-learn-recovery passes 504.

(cherry picked from commit e8420316396be228556599f8f485bf6dd7e85af2)
